'''Ehime Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|愛媛県}}, ''Ehime-ken'') is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located on the southwestern coast of the island of [[Ishijima]], facing the [[Katsuran Sea]] to the west. Its population as of 2026 was 1,232,562, with approximately 38% concentrated in the prefectural capital of [[Ehime, Ehime|Ehime]] (pop. 503,086). Ehime occupies a broad swathe of the Ishijima interior and western seaboard, stretching from the Katsuran Sea coast through the central highlands to the borders of [[Tochigi Prefecture|Tochigi]], [[Shiga Prefecture|Shiga]], and [[Haruno Prefecture|Haruno]].

Ehime is known throughout Okaiken for three things: its [[kabosu]] citrus, which is the prefecture's iconic agricultural product and the basis of its distinctive spicy cuisine; the {{nihongo|'''Ehime Tezutsu Hanabi'''|愛媛手筒花火}}, a festival in which participants hold firework cannons against their bodies as sparks cascade overhead; and its manufacturing sector, which is anchored by [[Ehime Laser Systems]] and the [[Kamigawa]] consumer appliance corridor. The prefecture's position on the Katsuran Sea provides access to the western maritime trade routes, while its interior highlands support extensive kiwi and kabosu orchards that thrive in the warm, wet tropical climate.

== History ==

=== Ancient and pre-shogunate period ===
The Ehime coast was originally settled by [[Atsu]] maritime communities who fished the Katsuran Sea and cultivated the narrow coastal plain. The sheltered harbours of the western coast—particularly the [[Ehime Bay]] inlet—provided natural anchorages for the [[Kajika]] wayfinding expansion, and the region became an early node in the Shendan trade network.

The interior highlands of Ehime were inhabited by Atsu highland communities who developed the cultivation of citrus fruits—particularly the [[kabosu]], a tart, aromatic green citrus that is unique to the Ehime highlands and thrives in the warm, wet conditions of the tropical monsoon climate. The kabosu was first domesticated in the Ehime interior around the 4th century CE, and its cultivation became the defining agricultural practice of the region.

=== The Honda Shogunate and the Ehime Domain ===
The [[Ehime Domain]] ({{lang|ja|愛媛藩}}, ''Ehime-han'') was established under the [[Honda Shogunate]] as a retainer territory of the [[Matsuda clan]], hereditary vassals of the Honda who controlled the southwestern approaches to the Ishijima interior. The domain's wealth was built on the kabosu trade—the fruit was prized at the shogunal court for its tart flavour and its aromatic oil, which was used in ceremonial preparations—and on the Katsuran Sea fishing grounds that supplied the interior with saba and other marine products.

The Shogunate period also saw the development of the {{nihongo|'''Tezutsu Hanabi'''|手筒花火}}, the firework cannon tradition that would become Ehime's most famous cultural practice. The festival originated as a Shinto purification ritual at the [[Suo Shrine]] on the Katsuran Sea coast, where shrine parishioners held bamboo tubes filled with gunpowder against their bodies as the sparks cascaded upward—a demonstration of courage and spiritual devotion that was believed to drive away evil spirits and ensure a bountiful harvest. The tradition spread throughout the Ehime coast during the shogunate period and is now practised at over 40 shrines across the prefecture.

=== Modern era ===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 converted the Ehime Domain into Ehime Prefecture. The new government recognised the strategic value of the Katsuran Sea coast for industrial development, and the [[Ehime Industrial Zone]] was established in 1888 along the western shoreline. The zone initially focused on textile production and food processing—using the abundant kabosu and kiwi crops of the interior—but diversified into metalworking and machinery in the early 20th century.

The post-[[Great Adonian War]] period saw Ehime Prefecture emerge as a centre for precision manufacturing. The establishment of [[Ehime Laser Systems]] in 1962, originally a spin-off from the [[Tochigi Precision Works]] optical cluster, marked the beginning of the prefecture's specialisation in laser technology. The [[Kamigawa]] consumer appliance corridor, which developed in the 1970s and 1980s, transformed the prefecture into one of the largest producers of household appliances on Ishijima.

== Geography ==

Ehime Prefecture occupies the southwestern quadrant of Ishijima, stretching from the [[Katsuran Sea]] coast in the west to the interior highlands that border [[Tochigi Prefecture|Tochigi]] in the east, [[Shiga Prefecture|Shiga]] in the north, and [[Haruno Prefecture|Haruno]] in the southeast. The prefecture's geography is defined by the transition from the narrow Katsuran Sea coastal plain through the central Ehime Hills to the elevated interior basins that support the kabosu and kiwi orchards.

The [[Ehime Hills]] ({{lang|ja|愛媛丘陵}}, ''Ehime-kyūryō''), a range of low, rounded ridges rising to 200–400 m, form the central spine of the prefecture. The hills are deeply incised by river valleys that drain westward into the Katsuran Sea, creating a series of sheltered, humid valleys that are ideal for citrus cultivation. The [[Ehime River]] ({{lang|ja|愛媛川}}, ''Ehime-gawa''), the prefecture's longest waterway at 62 km, flows from the interior highlands through the Ehime Hills to the coast at [[Ehime Bay]].

The [[Oshi Highlands]] ({{lang|ja|忍高原}}, ''Oshi-kōgen''), in the eastern reaches of the prefecture, rise to over 800 m and represent the transition zone between the Ehime Hills and the Asuka Range of neighbouring Tochigi. The highlands are cooler and wetter than the coast, supporting dense tropical forest and the upland kiwi orchards that have become a major agricultural product.

