== Economy ==

Ehime Prefecture's economy is built on two pillars: manufacturing and tropical agriculture. The Katsuran Sea coast supports a dense industrial corridor specialising in laser technology and consumer appliances, while the interior highlands produce some of Okaiken's most distinctive agricultural products—kabosu citrus, kiwifruit, and the spicy miso paste that defines the prefecture's cuisine.

=== Laser technology ===
[[Ehime Laser Systems]] ({{lang|ja|愛媛レーザーシステム}}, ''Ehime Rēzā Shisutemu''), headquartered in [[Ehime, Ehime|Ehime]], is the prefecture's largest company and one of Okaiken's leading manufacturers of industrial laser equipment. Founded in 1962 as a spin-off from the [[Tochigi Precision Works]] optical cluster, Ehime Laser Systems originally produced precision optical components for the semiconductor industry before pivoting to laser technology in the 1970s.

The company's product range includes high-power industrial cutting and welding lasers for the automotive and shipbuilding industries, medical laser systems for surgical and ophthalmic applications, and semiconductor lithography laser sources that are used by [[Kizan Systems]] foundries across Okaiken. Ehime Laser Systems employs approximately 4,800 workers at its Ehime campus and generates annual revenues of 円186 billion, making it the largest private employer in the prefecture.

The [[Ehime Laser Research Park]], a cluster of research laboratories and start-up incubators adjacent to the Ehime Laser Systems campus, has become one of the leading photonics research centres in Fosia. The park is affiliated with the [[Ehime Institute of Photonics]] and attracts researchers from across the Shendan region.

=== Consumer appliances ===
The {{nihongo|'''Kamigawa Appliance Corridor'''|上川家電回廊|Kamigawa Kaden Kairō}} is a 25-kilometre strip of manufacturing facilities along the Katsuran Sea coast centred on [[Kamigawa, Ehime|Kamigawa]]. The corridor is the second-largest consumer appliance manufacturing zone in Okaiken after the [[Yonezawa Industrial Zone]], producing approximately 15% of the nation's household appliances.

The corridor's anchor company is [[Kamigawa Electric]] ({{lang|ja|上川電機}}, ''Kamigawa Denki''), a major manufacturer of refrigerators, air conditioning units, and rice cookers that is one of the most recognised consumer brands in Okaiken. Kamigawa Electric was founded in 1954 and has grown into a 円420 billion company with approximately 12,000 employees across its Ehime manufacturing campus and its Okami corporate headquarters. The company's products are particularly known for their durability in tropical climates—a design philosophy that has made them the dominant appliance brand in the Shendan island markets.

Other manufacturers in the corridor include [[Suo Precision]] (microwave ovens and induction cooktops), [[Tabuse Electronics]] (small home appliances and electric kettles), and [[Abo Metalworks]] (stamped steel components for the appliance supply chain).

=== Kabosu and kiwi agriculture ===
The [[kabosu]] ({{lang|ja|カボス}}, ''kabosu'') is Ehime's signature agricultural product and one of the most distinctive citrus fruits in Okaiken. A tart, aromatic green citrus similar in appearance to a lime but with a unique floral fragrance, the kabosu is cultivated exclusively in the Ehime interior, where the warm, wet tropical climate and the deep volcanic soils of the Ehime Hills create ideal growing conditions. The fruit is harvested green in late summer and early autumn, and is used both fresh—as a garnish for sashimi, grilled fish, and spicy miso soup—and processed into juice, vinegar, and the aromatic kabosu oil that is used in perfumery and traditional medicine.

Ehime Prefecture produces approximately 14,000 tonnes of kabosu annually, representing over 95% of the national supply. The [[Ehime Kabosu Cooperative]], based in [[Oshi, Ehime|Oshi]], coordinates production, quality control, and marketing for the approximately 1,200 kabosu farms of the interior. The [[Ehime Kabosu Festival]], held in September in the Oshi Highlands, draws over 60,000 visitors and features kabosu-tasting competitions, kabosu-based cooking demonstrations, and the selection of the year's "Golden Kabosu" — the finest fruit of the harvest.

The [[Oshi kiwifruit]] ({{lang|ja|忍キウイ}}, ''Oshi-kiui'') is the prefecture's second major agricultural product. The Oshi Highlands, with their cooler temperatures and higher rainfall, provide ideal conditions for kiwifruit cultivation, and Ehime is the third-largest kiwifruit-producing prefecture in Okaiken. The [[Ehime Gold Kiwi]], a proprietary variety developed by the Ehime Agricultural Research Station, is a sweet, smooth-skinned gold kiwifruit that commands premium prices in both the domestic and Fosian markets.

=== Spicy miso ===
Ehime Prefecture is the origin of {{nihongo|'''Ehime karā-miso'''|愛媛辛味噌|Ehime karā-miso}}, a distinctive spicy miso paste that is one of the most recognised condiments in Okaiken. The paste is made by fermenting soybeans with [[kabosu]] juice, chili peppers, and the aromatic oils of the kabosu rind, producing a tart, fiery condiment that is used as a base for soups, a marinade for grilled fish, and a dipping sauce for the prefecture's celebrated saba dishes. The [[Ehime Miso Brewers' Association]], based in [[Jinseki, Ehime|Jinseki]], maintains production standards for the approximately 80 miso breweries that produce karā-miso in the traditional style.
Ehime's industrial economy is distinguished by high-performance composite manufacturing and maritime engineering. Specialized aerospace and marine textile mills weave ultra-high-molecular-weight carbon fibers used to fabricate lightweight pressure hulls for deep-submergence exploration vehicles and high-speed coastal catamarans. In tandem with automated laser cutting facilities, the prefecture supports a thriving citrus agro-industrial sector, where robotic processing plants extract cold-pressed essential oils from mikan peel for international fragrance and pharmaceutical exporters.

== Culture ==

=== The Tezutsu Hanabi ===
The {{nihongo|'''Ehime Tezutsu Hanabi'''|愛媛手筒花火}} is the prefecture's most iconic cultural practice and one of the most spectacular festivals in Okaiken. The tradition involves participants holding {{nihongo|'''tezutsu'''|手筒}}, cylindrical bamboo firework cannons approximately 80 cm long and 10 cm in diameter, against their bodies as the gunpowder inside ignites and sends a cascade of sparks shooting 10–15 metres into the air. The sparks rain down on the bearer, who stands motionless in a traditional loincloth and straw sandals, demonstrating courage and spiritual devotion.

The festival originated as a Shinto purification ritual at the [[Suo Shrine]] on the Katsuran Sea coast, where the tezutsu were believed to drive away evil spirits and ensure a bountiful kabosu harvest. The tradition spread throughout the Ehime coast during the Honda Shogunate and is now practised at over 40 shrines across the prefecture, with the largest events drawing over 30,000 spectators. The [[Suo Shrine Tezutsu Festival]], held on the last Saturday of July, is the most prestigious event and features over 200 tezutsu bearers in a three-hour display of fire and devotion.

=== Cuisine ===
Ehime Prefecture's cuisine is defined by the [[kabosu]] and the spicy miso tradition. The signature dish is {{nihongo|'''Ehime karā-miso saba'''|愛媛辛味噌鯖}}, a preparation of grilled saba mackerel basted in spicy kabosu miso paste, served with a wedge of fresh kabosu and a bowl of steamed rice. The dish is a staple of the Katsuran Sea coast restaurants and is considered one of the most distinctive preparations of the national fish in Okaiken.

Other local specialties include ''kabosu-zushi'' (vinegared rice topped with thinly sliced kabosu and sashimi), ''Oshi kiwi ice cream'' (made with the prefecture's gold kiwifruit), and ''Ehime ramen'' (a spicy miso-based ramen broth flavoured with kabosu juice and chili oil—a fiery counterpart to the milder ramen styles of the Ishijima coast).

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Ehime Line]] connects [[Ehime Station]] to the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] network at [[Shiga Station]], providing high-speed rail access to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]]. The [[Katsuran Southern Line]], a conventional rail service, runs along the western coast connecting the manufacturing towns of the Kamigawa corridor.

=== Maritime ===
The [[Port of Ehime]] is a medium-sized commercial harbour handling approximately 18 million tonnes of cargo annually. The port serves as the primary export terminal for the prefecture's kabosu, kiwifruit, and manufactured goods, and regular ferry services connect Ehime to the [[Ogasawara Islands]] and to ports along the Shendan coast.

== Education ==

The [[Ehime Institute of Photonics]] is the prefecture's premier research institution, specialising in laser physics, optical engineering, and photonics applications. The institute is affiliated with the [[Ehime Laser Research Park]] and maintains close ties with [[Ehime Laser Systems]]. The [[Ehime Agricultural Research Station]], based in the Oshi Highlands, is the nation's leading centre for kabosu and kiwifruit cultivation research.

